TRNG

(and rnd_attach_source will immediately trigger the jh7110_trng_get callback,
so you don't need to worry about writing logic yourself to prime the transfer)

But I do suggest you stash bytes_wanted as sc->sc_bytes_wanted,
and then decrement it and in the interrupt handler and reschedule
if not enough was returned.

You can test this by setting
    sysctl -w kern.entropy.gather=1
or
    sysctl -w kern.entropy.depletion=1

and then read from /dev/random or something.

skrll (@skrll:netbsd.org): sys/arch/arm/rockchip/rk_v1crypto.c
has an example of a sysctl (never returns the same data that it puts
into the entropy pool -- each sample is either returned by sysctl or
put in the entropy pool, but not both)



just to make sure it doesn't look obviously wrong, like all zeroes or
all 0101010101 or something


(and different each time)
(not that these possibilities all have zero probability, but the conditional
probability of a hardware failure or driver bug given that you see 128 zero
bits is a lot higher than if you see, say, 85c80bb2aa8a502194097ee0a4df96b4)


PCI
====

- {arm,riscv}_pci_msi_add
- {arm,riscv}_pci_msi

arch/riscv64/dev/stfrng.c:      return OF_is_compatible(faa->fa_node, "starfive,jh7110-trng");


